use Libraries.Web.Page.all
use Libraries.Web.Page.Standards.ComputerScienceStandards2026
use Libraries.Web.Page.Standards.QuorumStandards

/*
    Tutorial: Input Sets, part of the user interface series.
*/
class UserInterfacesInputSet is QuorumPage
    // The site's sets of standards, built once for every page.
    QuorumStandards standards

    on create
        SetLocation("tutorials/ui/inputSet.html")
        SetShortTitle("Input Sets")
        SetSectionName("User Interfaces")
    end

    action Setup
        // For search engines and link previews.
        SetDescription("How to use input sets and input tables in Quorum to add your own keys to a game, give different items different keys, choose when a behavior runs, and keep the keys keyboard and screen reader users depend on.")

        Title("Input Sets")

        Overview()
        WarmUp()
        Explore()
        Engage()
        WrapUp()
    end

    action Overview
        Section("Overview")
        Paragraph("Every program reacts to input. A person presses a key, clicks the mouse or
taps a screen, and something happens. Quorum already knows what most keys
should do in its controls: "):Key("Tab"):Text(" moves the focus, and "):Key("Space"):Text(" presses a
button. An "):Strong("input set"):Text(" describes one kind of input, such as "):DoubleQuote():Text("the "):Key("Space"):Text("
key is pressed"):DoubleQuote():Text(". An "):Strong("input table"):Text(" connects input sets to the
"):Strong("behaviors"):Text(" that should run. In this tutorial, you will use input sets
and input tables to add your own keys to a game, give different items
different keys, and choose exactly when a behavior runs. You will also
learn how to add keys without taking away the keys that keyboard and screen
reader users depend on.")

        Subsection("Goals")
        Paragraph("You have the following goals for this tutorial:")
        Goals()
        ListItem("Create an "):Code("InputSet"):Text(" for a key or mouse button and connect it to a behavior with an "):Code("InputTable")
        ListItem("Use input groups so that different items respond to the same key in different ways")
        ListItem("Use triggers, modifier keys and event counts to control when a behavior runs")
        ListItem("Add new keys without breaking the keys people already use to move around a program")
    end

    action WarmUp
        Section("Warm up")
        Paragraph("Think of a game or app you use with a keyboard. Which keys does it use, and
how did you learn them? Does the same key do different things in different
places, like "):Key("Enter"):Text(" in a text box and "):Key("Enter"):Text(" on a button? If you could
change one key in that program, which would it be and why? Who might need
to change a key for a reason you have not thought of?")

        Subsection("Vocabulary")
        Paragraph("You will be learning about the following vocabulary words:")
        Table("Vocabulary", "media/userInterface/InputSet/Vocabulary.csv")

        Subsection("New Code to Learn")
        Paragraph("You will be using the following new pieces of code:")
        Table("New Code to Learn", "media/userInterface/InputSet/Code.csv")

        Subsection("CSTA Standards")
        Paragraph("This tutorial covers the following "):Link("CSTA standards", "https://csteachers.org/pk12standards/view/"):Text(":")
        ComputerScienceStandards2026 computerScience = standards:GetComputerScience2026()
        CoversStandard(computerScience, computerScience:HIGH_SCHOOL_PROGRAM_DEVELOPMENT_12)
        CoversStandard(computerScience, computerScience:MIDDLE_SCHOOL_PROGRAM_DEVELOPMENT_12)
        CoversStandard(computerScience, computerScience:SPECIALTY_1_SOFTWARE_DEVELOPMENT_TESTING_AND_REFINING_7)
        CoversStandard(computerScience, computerScience:MIDDLE_SCHOOL_ALGORITHMIC_PROBLEM_SOLVING_2)
        StandardsList(computerScience)
    end

    action Explore
        Section("Explore")
        HowQuorumDecides()
        ABehaviorToRun()
        AddingAKeyToTheDefaultTable()
        GivingEachItemItsOwnKeys()
        WhichTableWins()
        OneBehaviorMoreThanOneKey()
        Triggers()
        ModifierKeys()
        DoubleClicksAndEventCounts()
        KeysThatEveryoneCanUse()
    end

    private action HowQuorumDecides
        Subsection("How Quorum decides what a key does")
        Paragraph("When you press a key, Quorum looks at the item that has the "):Strong("focus"):Text(", which
you learned about in the "):Link("Focus", "/tutorials/ui/focus.html"):Text(" tutorial. Quorum
then checks input tables in this order:")
        NumberedList()
        ListItem("The input table whose name matches the focused item's "):Strong("input group"):Text(".")
        ListItem("If that table has nothing for this key, the input tables of the item's
parent, then its parent's parent, and so on.")
        ListItem("If none of those has anything for this key, the "):Strong("default input
table"):Text(".")

        Paragraph("As soon as one table runs a behavior, Quorum stops looking. This is why the
same key can do different things in different places. Quorum's own controls
work this way. Every "):Code("Button"):Text(" has the input group "):DoubleQuote():Text("Button"):DoubleQuote():Text(", and the
"):DoubleQuote():Text("Button"):DoubleQuote():Text(" table runs the button's behavior when you press "):Key("Space"):Text(" or
"):Key("Enter"):Text(". The default input table is where "):Key("Tab"):Text(" and "):Keys("Shift", "Tab"):Text(" live: they
move the focus to the next or previous item in every game. It also holds
"):Keys("Shift", "F1"):Text(", which shows the tooltip of the control that has the focus, and
"):Key("Escape"):Text(", which closes it.")

        Paragraph("The programs in this tutorial use the "):Code("Game"):Text(" class rather than a form. The
controls on a form already come with their keys, and input tables are most
useful when you draw your own items, as games do. You will make two shapes
and give them keys of their own.")

        Paragraph("A "):Strong("behavior"):Text(" is the code that runs. You may remember behaviors from the
"):Link("Behaviors tutorial", "/tutorials/ui/behavior.html"):Text(". Every behavior has a
"):Code("Run"):Text(" action, and the "):Code("BehaviorEvent"):Text(" it receives tells you which item had
the focus.")
    end

    private action ABehaviorToRun
        Subsection("A behavior to run")
        Paragraph("Your first behavior writes out the name of the focused item. Add a new
class named "):Code("OutputBehavior.quorum"):Text(":")
        CodeExampleFromFile("sources/tutorials/ui/InputSet/OutputBehavior.quorum")
    end

    private action AddingAKeyToTheDefaultTable
        Subsection("Adding a key to the default input table")
        Paragraph("Now make the game in "):Code("Main.quorum"):Text(". It has two shapes, an orange square and
a purple circle. Each shape gets a name and a description, so a screen
reader can say what it is. Shapes are not read by screen readers unless you
give them an "):Strong("accessibility code"):Text(", so each shape also gets the code
"):Code("ITEM"):Text(". Each shape can take the focus, and "):Key("Tab"):Text(" moves between them.")

        Paragraph("The new part is at the end. The program makes an input set for the "):Key("Space"):Text("
key, gets the default input table, and adds the input set with an
"):Code("OutputBehavior"):Text(".")
        CodeExampleFromFile("sources/tutorials/ui/InputSet/Main.quorum")

        Paragraph("Run the program and press "):Key("Space"):Text(". The output says "):DoubleQuote():Text("Orange square has the
focus."):DoubleQuote():Text(" Press "):Key("Tab"):Text(", then "):Key("Space"):Text(" again, and the output names the circle.
With a screen reader running, "):Key("Tab"):Text(" also makes the screen reader say the
shape's name.")

        Paragraph():Code("KeyboardEvent"):Text(" holds a constant for every key, such as "):Code("keys:SPACE"):Text(",
"):Code("keys:ENTER"):Text(" and "):Code("keys:R"):Text(". "):Code("ITEM"):Text(" is a constant of the "):Code("Item"):Text(" class, which
a "):Code("Drawable"):Text(" inherits from, so the program reaches it through
"):Code("square:parent:Item:ITEM"):Text(".")
    end

    private action GivingEachItemItsOwnKeys
        Subsection("Giving each item its own keys")
        Paragraph("The default input table is the same for every item. Often you want a key to
do something different depending on what has the focus. In this part, the
square turns when you press "):Key("Space"):Text(", and the circle grows or shrinks when
you press "):Key("Enter"):Text(".")

        Paragraph("Add two more behaviors to your project. The first turns the focused item by
45 degrees. Put it in "):Code("RotationBehavior.quorum"):Text(":")
        CodeExampleFromFile("sources/tutorials/ui/InputSet/RotationBehavior.quorum")

        Paragraph("The second makes the item bigger, then back to normal, each time it runs.
Put it in "):Code("ScaleBehavior.quorum"):Text(":")
        CodeExampleFromFile("sources/tutorials/ui/InputSet/ScaleBehavior.quorum")

        Paragraph("Now connect them. Three steps do it:")
        NumberedList()
        ListItem("Give each shape an "):Strong("input group"):Text(" with "):Code("SetInputGroup"):Text(".")
        ListItem("Make an input table for each group, and name it with "):Code("SetIdentifier"):Text(".
The name must match the input group exactly.")
        ListItem("Add each table to the game with "):Code("AddInputTable"):Text(". The default input table
is already part of the game, but new tables must be added.")

        Paragraph("Here is the whole "):Code("Main.quorum"):Text(". The descriptions now tell a person which
key to press:")
        CodeExampleFromFile("sources/tutorials/ui/InputSet/Main2.quorum")

        BlockEditorFile("OutputBehavior.quorum", "html/sources/tutorials/ui/InputSet/OutputBehavior.quorum")
        BlockEditorFile("RotationBehavior.quorum", "html/sources/tutorials/ui/InputSet/RotationBehavior.quorum")
        BlockEditorFile("ScaleBehavior.quorum", "html/sources/tutorials/ui/InputSet/ScaleBehavior.quorum")
        BlockEditor("Main.quorum", "html/sources/tutorials/ui/InputSet/Main2.quorum", "", true, true)
    end

    private action WhichTableWins
        Subsection("Which table wins?")
        Paragraph("Try these keys and compare what happens with the order from the start of
this tutorial:")
        BulletList()
        ListItem():Key("Space"):Strong(" on the square:"):Text(" the square turns. The "):DoubleQuote():Text("Square"):DoubleQuote():Text("
table has "):Key("Space"):Text(", so Quorum stops there. "):Code("OutputBehavior"):Text(" does not run.")
        ListItem():Key("Space"):Strong(" on the circle:"):Text(" the output names the circle. The "):DoubleQuote():Text("Circle"):DoubleQuote():Text("
table has no "):Key("Space"):Text(", so Quorum falls back to the default input table.")
        ListItem():Key("Enter"):Strong(" on the circle:"):Text(" the circle grows, or shrinks back.")
        ListItem():Key("Enter"):Strong(" on the square:"):Text(" nothing happens. No table has "):Key("Enter"):Text(" for the
square.")
        ListItem():Key("Tab"):Strong(":"):Text(" the focus moves. Neither new table has "):Key("Tab"):Text(", so the default
input table still handles it.")
    end

    private action OneBehaviorMoreThanOneKey
        Subsection("One behavior, more than one key")
        Paragraph("An input table can hold many input sets, and two input sets can run the
same behavior. This is how a program offers a second key for the same job,
or lets a person choose their own keys. Add these lines to "):Code("CreateGame"):Text(", and
the "):Key("R"):Text(" key also turns the square:")
        CodeExampleFromFile("sources/tutorials/ui/InputSet/RKey.quorum")

        Paragraph("To take a key away, use the table's "):Code("RemoveKey"):Text(" action with an input set for
that key.")
    end

    private action Triggers
        Subsection("Triggers: when a behavior runs")
        Paragraph("Input has a beginning, a middle and an end. A key goes down, is held, and
comes back up. The "):Strong("trigger"):Text(" chooses which of these moments runs the
behavior. The triggers are constants in "):Code("InputSet"):Text(":")
        BulletList()
        ListItem():Code("BEGIN"):Strong(":"):Text(" when the input starts, such as when a key is first pressed.
This is the trigger an input set uses if you do not choose one.")
        ListItem():Code("CONTINUE"):Strong(":"):Text(" on every frame while the input goes on, such as while a
key is held down. This is useful for moving a character for as long as an
arrow key is held.")
        ListItem():Code("FINISH"):Strong(":"):Text(" when the input ends, such as when a key is let go.")
        ListItem():Code("FINISH_UNCAUGHT"):Strong(":"):Text(" for keys only. When a key is let go, but only if it
was not used as part of a key combination.")

        Paragraph("Use "):Code("SetInputTrigger"):Text(" to choose one. Add these lines to "):Code("CreateGame"):Text(". Now
the square keeps spinning for as long as the "):Key("Right Arrow"):Text(" key is held
down:")
        CodeExampleFromFile("sources/tutorials/ui/InputSet/HoldRight.quorum")
    end

    private action ModifierKeys
        Subsection("Modifier keys")
        Paragraph("A "):Strong("modifier key"):Text(" is held down while you press another key or click. The
modifiers are constants in "):Code("InputSet"):Text(":")
        BulletList()
        ListItem():Code("SHIFT"):Text(";")
        ListItem():Code("CONTROL"):Text(", the "):Key("Ctrl"):Text(" key;")
        ListItem():Code("ALT"):Text(", which is the "):Key("Option"):Text(" key on a Mac;")
        ListItem():Code("META"):Text(", the "):Key("Command"):Text(" key on a Mac or the Windows key on Windows. Windows
keeps most Windows key combinations for itself, so avoid "):Code("META"):Text(" there.")

        Paragraph("Use "):Code("AddModifier"):Text(" to require one, and "):Code("RemoveModifier"):Text(" to take it away. This
input set runs a behavior on "):Key("Ctrl"):Text("+click with the left mouse button:")
        CodeExampleFromFile("sources/tutorials/ui/InputSet/ControlClick.quorum")

        Paragraph("A mouse input set needs a key that does the same thing, so it works from
the keyboard too. When you add "):Code("controlClick"):Text(" to a table, add this input
set for "):Keys("Ctrl", "Enter"):Text(" to the same table, with the same behavior:")
        CodeExampleFromFile("sources/tutorials/ui/InputSet/ControlEnter.quorum")

        Paragraph("An input set with a modifier is a different input set from one without it.
"):Keys("Shift", "Space"):Text(" and "):Key("Space"):Text(" can run different behaviors, even in the same
table.")
    end

    private action DoubleClicksAndEventCounts
        Subsection("Double-clicks and event counts")
        Paragraph("Some input only counts when it happens quickly more than once, like a
double-click. Use "):Code("SetEventCount"):Text(" to say how many times. This input set
describes a double-click with the left mouse button:")
        CodeExampleFromFile("sources/tutorials/ui/InputSet/DoubleClick.quorum")

        Paragraph("The count can be from 1 to 4. As with "):Key("Ctrl"):Text("+click, a double-click needs
a key too. Add "):Code("doubleClick"):Text(" and an input set for a key such as "):Key("Enter"):Text(" to
the same table with the same behavior, as in "):DoubleQuote():Text("One behavior, more than one
key"):DoubleQuote():Text(".")
    end

    private action KeysThatEveryoneCanUse
        Subsection("Keys that everyone can use")
        Paragraph("Input sets give you a lot of power over keys. Use it with care:")
        BulletList()
        ListItem():Strong("Leave "):Key("Tab"):Strong(" and "):Keys("Shift", "Tab"):Strong(" alone."):Text(" Keyboard and screen reader users
move through a program with them. If you add "):Key("Tab"):Text(" to a table, that item
may become a "):Strong("keyboard trap"):Text(": the focus goes in and cannot get out.")
        ListItem():Strong("Give every mouse action a key too."):Text(" Some people cannot use a mouse. If
a double-click opens something, a key such as "):Key("Enter"):Text(" should open it as
well.")
        ListItem():Strong("Say which keys to use."):Text(" Put the key in the item's description, as the
program above does. A screen reader reads the description, and you can
show the same words on screen.")
        ListItem():Strong("Avoid keys Quorum already uses."):Text(" "):Key("Tab"):Text(", "):Keys("Shift", "Tab"):Text(", "):Keys("Shift", "F1"):Text(" and
"):Key("Escape"):Text(" are in the default input table. If you add one of them to that
table, your behavior takes its place in every part of the program.")
        ListItem():Strong("Avoid keys the computer already uses."):Text(" Screen readers use many keys
for themselves. For example, NVDA uses "):Key("Insert"):Text(" and VoiceOver uses
"):Keys("Ctrl", "Option"):Text(".")

        Paragraph("You can practice moving through programs without a mouse in
"):Link("No-Mouse Practice", "/tutorials/accessibility/nomousepractice.html"):Text(".")
    end

    action Engage
        Section("Engage")
        Paragraph("In this activity, you design a new shape with its own keys for the game on
this page, write a test plan for it before you try it, and then run the
plan. The goal is to set up input groups and input tables, and to plan
tests before trying a program. Add a third shape to the game, such as a
filled rectangle of a different color. Give it a name, a description, the
"):Code("ITEM"):Text(" accessibility code, and a place in the "):Key("Tab"):Text(" order. Then give the
new shape its own input group and input table, with two keys. At least one
key must use a modifier, such as "):Keys("Shift", "R"):Text(" to turn the other way with
"):Code("Rotate(-45)"):Text(".")

        Paragraph("Before you run the program, write a short test plan: for each shape, list
each key, what should happen, and what should not happen, including keys
that should do nothing. Then run the plan without touching the mouse, and
mark each line "):DoubleQuote():Text("pass"):DoubleQuote():Text(" or "):DoubleQuote():Text("fail"):DoubleQuote():Text(". Fix any failures and run the plan again.
Finally, add one change that lets a person use a second key for the same
job, and add lines to your plan to test it. Then draw "):DoubleQuote():Text("Which table wins?"):DoubleQuote():Text("
for one of your new keys as a flowchart or write it as pseudocode: which
input table Quorum checks first, what happens when a key matches, and what
happens when it doesn't. You may also be given a Parsons
problem that asks you to put the lines of an input table setup in the right
order.")
    end

    action WrapUp
        Section("Wrap up")
        Paragraph("Input sets and input tables let you decide what each key does, and where.
Go back to your test plan. Which test found a problem you did not expect?
If a person told you they could not press two keys at once, how would you
change your program for them?")
    end
end
